1
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:08,150
Legal documents face two quiet risks.

2
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:13,664
First, a file can be changed after everyone agreed to it, and nobody notices.

3
00:00:13,664 --> 00:00:18,247
Second, anyone with a login can upload or approve from anywhere.

4
00:00:18,247 --> 00:00:21,613
GeoLegalVault is a prototype that tackles both.

5
00:00:21,613 --> 00:00:25,981
Uploads and approvals are allowed only from an approved area.

6
00:00:25,981 --> 00:00:30,707
That area is called a geofence, a virtual boundary drawn on a map.

7
00:00:30,707 --> 00:00:37,511
Every document is reviewed and approved by different people, so nobody approves their own work.

8
00:00:37,511 --> 00:00:46,534
When a version is approved, a fingerprint of the file, called a SHA-256 hash, is written to the Ethereum Sepolia test network.

9
00:00:46,534 --> 00:00:52,478
Later, anyone can recompute the fingerprint and see whether the file still matches.

10
00:00:52,478 --> 00:00:56,559
This is tamper-evident, which means changes are detected.

11
00:00:56,559 --> 00:00:58,565
It does not prevent changes.

12
00:00:58,565 --> 00:01:02,217
Everything you will see is the real running system.

13
00:01:02,217 --> 00:01:09,020
Where the screen cannot show something, like database records, I label it a supplementary view.

14
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:12,500
First, login and roles.

15
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:20,327
There are five roles: Administrator, Legal Officer, Reviewing Officer, Authorized Staff and Auditor.

16
00:01:20,327 --> 00:01:22,754
A role is a set of permissions.

17
00:01:22,754 --> 00:01:25,493
Here Authorized Staff is signed in.

18
00:01:25,493 --> 00:01:30,033
The menu has Upload, but no Admin Panel and no Audit Logs.

19
00:01:30,033 --> 00:01:35,747
Next, a Legal Officer signs in, in a completely separate browser session.

20
00:01:35,747 --> 00:01:37,469
Then an Administrator.

21
00:01:37,469 --> 00:01:42,400
Their menu shows the Admin Panel and Audit Logs, but no Upload.

22
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:48,740
Administrators manage users and zones, but they cannot upload, review or approve.

23
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:51,871
That separation of duties is deliberate.

24
00:01:51,871 --> 00:01:59,307
Each user also has a clearance level: Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted or Top Secret.

25
00:01:59,307 --> 00:02:03,299
Clearance decides which documents a person may see.

26
00:02:03,299 --> 00:02:05,725
The menu is only a convenience.

27
00:02:05,725 --> 00:02:10,500
The server checks role and clearance on every single request.

28
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:19,427
Now the Administrator's Admin Panel, which has Users, Geofences, Reports and System Health.

29
00:02:19,427 --> 00:02:22,127
The Geofences tab lists the zones.

30
00:02:22,127 --> 00:02:27,448
A zone is a polygon, a shape made of longitude and latitude points.

31
00:02:27,448 --> 00:02:34,039
Our demo zone, HQ Campus, is a small box around latitude 11.67 and longitude 78.15.

32
00:02:34,039 --> 00:02:37,930
Every request is tested against it on the server.

33
00:02:37,930 --> 00:02:39,360
Now the Users tab.

34
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:43,489
We create a throwaway account with the Auditor role.

35
00:02:43,489 --> 00:02:50,160
A new user starts at the lowest clearance, Public, and the password field is masked.

36
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:52,622
We will use this account later.

37
00:02:52,622 --> 00:02:54,687
Login also has protection.

38
00:02:54,687 --> 00:02:59,928
On this throwaway account only, I type wrong passwords on purpose.

39
00:02:59,928 --> 00:03:09,140
After five failures the account is locked for a short time, and the next attempt is refused with: Too many attempts.

40
00:03:09,140 --> 00:03:11,840
That slows down password guessing.

41
00:03:13,530 --> 00:03:14,904
Now the main flow.

42
00:03:14,904 --> 00:03:17,193
Authorized Staff opens Upload.

43
00:03:17,193 --> 00:03:20,856
The page shows the location the browser reports.

44
00:03:20,856 --> 00:03:26,121
On a real device that comes from the browser's GPS or Wi-Fi location.

45
00:03:26,121 --> 00:03:31,311
In this recording a test harness supplies it, and the label says so.

46
00:03:31,311 --> 00:03:33,600
The server still validates it.

47
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:36,347
This location is inside the HQ zone.

48
00:03:36,347 --> 00:03:42,528
We enter a title, a type, the classification Confidential, and pick a sample PDF.

49
00:03:42,528 --> 00:03:50,160
On Upload, the server checks, in a fixed order: your token, your role, your location, then the file.

50
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:55,425
Then it stores the file, computes the hash and writes an audit entry.

51
00:03:55,425 --> 00:03:57,943
The document is saved as a Draft.

52
00:03:57,943 --> 00:04:07,024
In this supplementary view, the real database shows the document row, and the version row with the SHA-256 fingerprint.

53
00:04:07,024 --> 00:04:10,000
The file itself is not in the database.

54
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:20,226
It sits in private object storage, called RustFS here and Cloudflare R2 in production, under a server-made key that includes the hash.

55
00:04:20,226 --> 00:04:25,873
The uploader then submits it for review, and the status becomes Submitted.

56
00:04:25,873 --> 00:04:30,528
In the app, Version History shows the fingerprint, shortened.

57
00:04:30,528 --> 00:04:37,549
A SHA-256 hash turns any file into 64 characters, and changing one byte changes all of them.

58
00:04:37,549 --> 00:04:41,059
The bucket listing confirms the stored object.

59
00:04:41,059 --> 00:04:42,738
The bucket is private.

60
00:04:42,738 --> 00:04:46,630
Downloads use a link that expires in sixty seconds.

61
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:50,132
Now the negative cases.

62
00:04:50,132 --> 00:04:57,606
The same user tries again, but the browser reports a place about 100 kilometres outside the zone.

63
00:04:57,606 --> 00:05:02,076
The server answers GEOFENCE_DENIED, and nothing is stored.

64
00:05:02,076 --> 00:05:06,391
The server decides inside or outside, never the browser.

65
00:05:06,391 --> 00:05:12,709
Now the location is inside the zone, but very imprecise: plus or minus 500 metres.

66
00:05:12,709 --> 00:05:17,178
The server refuses that too, with LOCATION_LOW_CONFIDENCE.

67
00:05:17,178 --> 00:05:22,264
This is called fail-closed: if the system is not sure, it says no.

68
00:05:22,264 --> 00:05:26,270
This machine allows up to 300 metres of uncertainty.

69
00:05:26,270 --> 00:05:33,745
In the supplementary view, the zone denial is written to the audit log with the claimed position.

70
00:05:33,745 --> 00:05:39,986
An honest limit: browser location can be faked, for example with developer tools.

71
00:05:39,986 --> 00:05:44,378
So geofencing here is a policy control that adds a layer.

72
00:05:44,378 --> 00:05:46,073
It is not a guarantee.

73
00:05:46,073 --> 00:05:51,390
The tamper-evidence comes from the blockchain, not from the geofence.

74
00:05:53,090 --> 00:05:55,017
Next, review and approval.

75
00:05:55,017 --> 00:06:02,278
This uses maker-checker, meaning the person who made a document must not be the one who checks it.

76
00:06:02,278 --> 00:06:07,094
A Legal Officer uploads a second document, Doc B, and submits it.

77
00:06:07,094 --> 00:06:11,762
A Reviewing Officer, a different person, reviews and passes it.

78
00:06:11,762 --> 00:06:16,356
Back as the uploader, the Legal Officer has no Approve button.

79
00:06:16,356 --> 00:06:23,914
If they call the server directly anyway, from inside the zone, the answer is: MAKER_CHECKER_VIOLATION.

80
00:06:23,914 --> 00:06:27,990
That is a real response, shown in a supplementary view.

81
00:06:27,990 --> 00:06:29,694
Now for the main lease.

82
00:06:29,694 --> 00:06:34,881
The same Reviewing Officer passes it, and it becomes Pending Approval.

83
00:06:34,881 --> 00:06:39,178
Then a Legal Officer who did not upload it clicks Approve.

84
00:06:39,178 --> 00:06:41,401
The location is checked first.

85
00:06:41,401 --> 00:06:46,662
Approval starts anchoring automatically, and there is no anchor button.

86
00:06:46,662 --> 00:06:51,330
The backend's own wallet signs the transaction, never the user.

87
00:06:51,330 --> 00:06:57,999
This is a real transaction on the Sepolia test network, the free test version of Ethereum.

88
00:06:57,999 --> 00:07:01,037
We wait for it to be included in a block.

89
00:07:01,037 --> 00:07:02,963
The status becomes Active.

90
00:07:02,963 --> 00:07:08,891
Approval spent a tiny amount of test Ether, about five trillionths of one Ether.

91
00:07:08,891 --> 00:07:11,040
Test Ether has no real value.

92
00:07:12,690 --> 00:07:15,714
Now, what exactly went on the blockchain.

93
00:07:15,714 --> 00:07:24,637
The Blockchain Anchor Record shows the network, sepolia, the contract address, the transaction hash and the block number.

94
00:07:24,637 --> 00:07:31,201
Below it, the app asks the contract live, and the on-chain hash equals the recorded hash.

95
00:07:31,201 --> 00:07:41,969
The smart contract, DocumentAnchor, stores only four things per document version: the hash, the version number, an event type, and the block time.

96
00:07:41,969 --> 00:07:46,467
The PDF, its filename and any person's name are not on-chain.

97
00:07:46,467 --> 00:07:52,146
A blockchain is public and permanent, so we keep confidential content off it.

98
00:07:52,146 --> 00:07:56,792
The contract also refuses a second anchor for the same version.

99
00:07:56,792 --> 00:08:04,167
On the public Etherscan page, the transaction shows Success on the Sepolia testnet, with a tiny fee.

100
00:08:04,167 --> 00:08:08,076
One service wallet in the backend signs every anchor.

101
00:08:08,076 --> 00:08:11,690
There is no Merkle tree: each file gets one hash.

102
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:14,483
Now clearance.

103
00:08:14,483 --> 00:08:20,710
The throwaway Auditor has the lowest level, Public, and the lease is Confidential.

104
00:08:20,710 --> 00:08:28,228
In the repository the lease is simply not listed, because the server filters in the database query.

105
00:08:28,228 --> 00:08:32,632
Opening its exact link shows: that item couldn't be found.

106
00:08:32,632 --> 00:08:40,074
In the supplementary view, the real document and a made-up id give the same answer, 404 not found.

107
00:08:40,074 --> 00:08:47,896
We use 404 instead of 403, which means forbidden, because a 403 would confirm that the document exists.

108
00:08:47,896 --> 00:08:54,882
The refused attempt on the real document is still written to the audit log as ACCESS_DENIED.

109
00:08:54,882 --> 00:08:58,375
This is access control inside the application.

110
00:08:58,375 --> 00:08:59,970
It is not encryption.

111
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:04,333
Now the core feature: verification.

112
00:09:04,333 --> 00:09:08,365
When we run it, the server does three things in order.

113
00:09:08,365 --> 00:09:12,248
It reads the stored file and recomputes its SHA-256.

114
00:09:12,248 --> 00:09:16,951
It compares that with the hash saved in the database at upload.

115
00:09:16,951 --> 00:09:21,655
And it reads the anchored hash from the Sepolia contract, live.

116
00:09:21,655 --> 00:09:25,463
All three are identical, so the result is VERIFIED.

117
00:09:25,463 --> 00:09:35,020
A database change alone, or a storage change alone, cannot produce a false VERIFIED, because the chain holds the original value.

118
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:37,792
Now the attack.

119
00:09:37,792 --> 00:09:44,868
Someone with direct access to the storage server changes one byte of the file, not through the app.

120
00:09:44,868 --> 00:09:48,298
This script bypasses the application on purpose.

121
00:09:48,298 --> 00:09:52,658
The database and the blockchain still hold the original hash.

122
00:09:52,658 --> 00:09:54,516
We run verification again.

123
00:09:54,516 --> 00:09:57,375
The result is MISMATCH, tamper detected.

124
00:09:57,375 --> 00:10:04,093
The recomputed hash is completely different, while the stored and on-chain hashes still agree.

125
00:10:04,093 --> 00:10:07,524
One changed byte gives a totally different hash.

126
00:10:07,524 --> 00:10:10,096
That is called the avalanche effect.

127
00:10:10,096 --> 00:10:12,169
The document is also flagged.

128
00:10:12,169 --> 00:10:20,531
For the dev data, I then write the original bytes back to the same storage key, and verification returns to VERIFIED.

129
00:10:20,531 --> 00:10:24,462
Notice what the system did: it made the change evident.

130
00:10:24,462 --> 00:10:26,034
It did not prevent it.

131
00:10:26,034 --> 00:10:33,610
The integrity flag stays until an Administrator clears it, and clearing it re-runs the three checks first.

132
00:10:35,250 --> 00:10:37,671
There are six possible results.

133
00:10:37,671 --> 00:10:39,468
Here is one more, live.

134
00:10:39,468 --> 00:10:44,780
Doc B passed review but was never approved, so nothing was anchored.

135
00:10:44,780 --> 00:10:46,889
The result is NOT ANCHORED.

136
00:10:46,889 --> 00:10:52,356
The file is intact, but there is no blockchain record to compare with.

137
00:10:52,356 --> 00:10:55,325
That is not an error and not an alarm.

138
00:10:55,325 --> 00:10:58,527
The remaining outcomes are on this slide.

139
00:10:58,527 --> 00:11:00,324
MISMATCH, which we saw.

140
00:11:00,324 --> 00:11:05,948
ANCHOR_MISSING: the database says it was anchored but the chain says no.

141
00:11:05,948 --> 00:11:08,916
FILE_MISSING: the stored file is gone.

142
00:11:08,916 --> 00:11:15,712
And CHAIN_UNREACHABLE: the blockchain could not be read, so there is no verdict at all.

143
00:11:15,712 --> 00:11:21,570
I did not trigger those three live, because that would break the dev setup.

144
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:25,756
Finally, where everything lives.

145
00:11:25,756 --> 00:11:28,697
The Auditor opens the Audit Logs page.

146
00:11:28,697 --> 00:11:33,185
Every action is a row: who, what, to what, and the result.

147
00:11:33,185 --> 00:11:35,119
Rows are only ever added.

148
00:11:35,119 --> 00:11:47,268
In the first supplementary view, these are the audit rows for our lease: upload, submit, review, approve, anchor confirmed, activate, and the verify results.

149
00:11:47,268 --> 00:11:49,590
Next, the MongoDB collections.

150
00:11:49,590 --> 00:11:55,548
Users, geofences, documents and document versions are the authoritative data.

151
00:11:55,548 --> 00:12:01,274
The anchors collection is derived, because the chain is the real evidence.

152
00:12:01,274 --> 00:12:04,988
Verification records and audit logs are history.

153
00:12:04,988 --> 00:12:14,661
The ids link them: a version points to its document, an anchor points to its version, and the storage key points to the file.

154
00:12:14,661 --> 00:12:15,899
Last, the split.

155
00:12:15,899 --> 00:12:19,304
MongoDB holds state, fingerprints and audit.

156
00:12:19,304 --> 00:12:22,399
The private bucket holds the file bytes.

157
00:12:22,399 --> 00:12:27,506
The Sepolia contract holds the hash, version, event type and time.

158
00:12:27,506 --> 00:12:35,709
One honest gap: the audit log has no hash chain yet, so someone with database write access could alter it.

159
00:12:35,709 --> 00:12:38,030
That is a planned improvement.

160
00:12:39,710 --> 00:12:41,468
To summarise the parts.

161
00:12:41,468 --> 00:12:43,226
The front end is React.

162
00:12:43,226 --> 00:12:45,825
The back end is FastAPI in Python.

163
00:12:45,825 --> 00:12:47,660
MongoDB stores the data.

164
00:12:47,660 --> 00:12:53,164
RustFS in development, and Cloudflare R2 in production, store the files.

165
00:12:53,164 --> 00:12:58,591
web3.py talks to a Solidity smart contract on the Sepolia test network.

166
00:12:58,591 --> 00:13:03,101
Docker runs the services, and a CI pipeline runs the tests.

167
00:13:03,101 --> 00:13:17,472
Future work includes the open items in the project's own tracker: stronger login and secret handling, deployment hardening, a hash chain for the audit log, and malware scanning of uploads.

168
00:13:17,472 --> 00:13:25,117
There is also a planned next version that reduces how much you must trust the single service wallet.

169
00:13:25,117 --> 00:13:27,639
I won't describe its design here.

170
00:13:27,639 --> 00:13:34,596
Not demonstrated: document amendment with a second version, and the optional anchor worker.

171
00:13:34,596 --> 00:13:35,360
Thank you.
